%S A402123 1,0,0,1,0,1,0,0,1,0,1,0,1,0,1,1,1,0,0,2,1,2,0,1,1,2,0,3,1,2,1,0,2,2,
%T A402123 3,2,4,0,3,1,2,1,5,2,6,3,3,3,4,1,2,7,4,7,3,9,3,6,2,4,3,9,2,11,6,13,6,
%U A402123 9,5,8,5,2,10,8,13,10,16,9,20,8,16,3,12,4
%N A402123 Number of ways to partition the numbers from 1 to n into blocks of consecutive integers such that the sum of the smallest and largest numbers in each block is a perfect square.
%C A402123 A block consisting of a single number k is allowed when 2k is a perfect square, e.g., [2] and [8].
%C A402123 a(n) = 0 if and only if n is one of 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 11, 13, 17, 18, 22, 26, 31, 37. This can be proved by strong induction: for n >= 62, there is always a square in [n+39, 2n], leaving a remainder of at least 38; smaller n are checked directly.
%C A402123 Each such partition, upon reversing each block, gives a permutation p of 1..n such that i + p(i) is a square for every i; therefore a(n) <= A095986(n).
%H A402123 Zülfü Mirza Gür, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24334782">On permutations pi of {1,...,n} with i+pi(i) a perfect square</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A402123 a(n) = Sum_{k: n+1 <= k^2 <= 2n} a(k^2 - n - 1) for n >= 1, with a(0) = 1.
%F A402123 log(Sum_{k=0..n-1} a(k)) ~ sqrt(2)*log(phi)*sqrt(n), where phi = (1+sqrt(5))/2 = A001622 (the golden ratio).
%F A402123 limsup_{n->oo} log(a(n))/sqrt(n) = sqrt(2)*log(phi) = 0.680536..., with phi as above.
%e A402123 a(10) = 1: the only partition is [1..3], [4, 5], [6..10], with sums 1+3 = 4, 4+5 = 9, 6+10 = 16.
%e A402123 a(19) = 2: the two partitions are [1..3], [4, 5], [6..19], with sums 4, 9, 25; and [1..8], [9..16], [17..19], with sums 9, 25, 36.
%o A402123 (Python)
%o A402123 from math import isqrt
%o A402123 def lista(N):
%o A402123     b = [1]
%o A402123     for n in range(1, N + 1):
%o A402123         b.append(sum(b[k*k - n - 1] for k in range(isqrt(n) + 1, isqrt(2*n) + 1)))
%o A402123     return b
%o A402123 print(lista(83))
%Y A402123 Cf. A001622, A095986.
%K A402123 nonn,new
%O A402123 0,20
